[{:db/id "q-333886/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "bd13e9ce-3053-5427-b7b4-7a9aa4eb5f27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333886/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "022c80b9-e8ab-52c2-b507-e12459b28b41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333886/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-333886/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333886/field-2/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "77002463-bb1b-5129-b90a-a6405aef5f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333886/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d56ba329-5d4b-5c2a-9bba-31ba0b2cd448" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333886/field-2/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-333886/field-2/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333886" :question/id #uuid "be41030e-0dd1-5aa2-85fa-d47ccaa7a076" :question/math-academy-id "q-333886" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}},\\, & y(0)=4 \\\\ {z}^{′}=xy+2z,\\, & z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $z(2).$\n\n$\\,y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2\\cdot 4-2}{(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+8-2}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy+2z \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+3=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |  |  | $10$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈7$\nand\n$z(2)≈10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333886/field-1" "q-333886/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c9c14c-62d1-592c-81f7-92d683050e3c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333886"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "0bc0f595-454b-533d-9d37-67f8dda76aa1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "f57c031e-fe24-566e-b230-cd2688e86c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b71ff0c8-9718-58fd-b42f-09615d7d5c12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333890/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-333890/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-333890/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "bdbda780-2738-52e7-8078-ae4ef307b1a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "34a022ef-3365-52e5-ae1e-da0629ca5c01" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333890/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer-field/choices ["q-333890/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333890" :question/id #uuid "d1333f89-98cf-5c81-818c-4d85f314587c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333890" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3x-y+2z,\\, & y(1)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2x+y+z,\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$\n\n$\\,y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(3)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+2z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-3+2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x+y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3+1 \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+4=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+12=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $13$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n$z(3)≈13.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333890/field-1" "q-333890/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c9c14c-62d1-592c-81f7-92d683050e3c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333890"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333813/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "3074d1ec-ce8a-590b-8999-f6ab84959091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164484 :answer-field/correct "q-333813/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-333813/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333813/field-2/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "dbb53dc7-dafc-5e3c-856c-726d17747e00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164482 :answer-field/correct "q-333813/field-2/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-333813/field-2/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333813/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "a94935ca-b2f0-582a-8b5f-6430e4ee3688" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164485 :answer-field/correct "q-333813/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-333813/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333813/field-4/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "3f9ff048-07dc-5567-9b1c-fea8c7f24c50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164483 :answer-field/correct "q-333813/field-4/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-333813/field-4/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333813"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+y-z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+4y+z,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+1-0 \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+4y+z \\\\  & =0+4\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =0+4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $0$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+8=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3$ and $z(2)≈8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333823/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "14fcacbe-d39d-5143-b386-1efa6c007