idate]} {:db/id "q-335310/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "e095ed3f-ba20-5693-93f0-57a3a522d937" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335310/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-335310/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335310" :question/id #uuid "58db3156-6eea-531d-8a9b-6c540758be6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335310" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1-2y+z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2+x-w,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=yz,\\, & w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=2,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-2y+z \\\\  & =1-2\\cdot 0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2+x-w \\\\  & =2+0-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =yz \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+0=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335310/field-1" "q-335310/field-2" "q-335310/field-3" "q-335310/field-4" "q-335310/field-5" "q-335310/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8b20277-7fb3-5702-a295-1b1722d1cc5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335310"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21655"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+5y-z,\\, & y(0)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=5x-4y+2z,\\, & z(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+5y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 3-4 \\\\  & =0+15-4 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =5x-4y+2z \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0-12+8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ |  | $4$ | $-4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =11\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+11=14 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+(-4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $14$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx 14$\nand\n$z(1) \\approx 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21656"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & y(1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=3x+y-z,\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = \\dfrac22 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=1-1+2\\cdot 0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 1+1-0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=2-1+2\\cdot 4=9 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 2+1-4=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+9=10 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $9$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 10$\nand\n$z(3) \\approx 7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21660"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w=w(x){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2y+z+x,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=y+z+2x,\\, & z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′}=wy-x,\\, & w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproximate values of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x = 1$ using Euler's method if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=0,$ and $w(0)=2,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=0,$ and $w=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y', z',$ and $w'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y+z+x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+0+0 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y+z+2x \\\\  & =1+0+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =wy-x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ |  | $2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y,$ $\\Delta z,$ and $\\Delta w$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ and $\\Delta w$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+1=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ | $w$ | $w'$ | $\\Delta w$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3},$\n$z(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1},$\nand\n$w(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{4}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
